\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We define the formalization task (\S\ref{sec:method:task}), the GRPO training setup with execution-only reward (\S\ref{sec:method:grpo}), the Solver Trace Ablation (STA) protocol (\S\ref{sec:method:sta}) and its derived metric ISR (\S\ref{sec:method:isr}), the family of reward formulations we compare (\S\ref{sec:method:rewards}), and the seven-mode hacking taxonomy used to characterize policy outputs (\S\ref{sec:method:taxonomy}).

\subsection{Task}
\label{sec:method:task}

A formalizer is a policy $\pi_\theta$ that maps a natural-language premise set with a target conclusion $x = (P_{\text{NL}}, q_{\text{NL}})$ to a formal program $y = (P_{\text{F}}, q_{\text{F}})$ in a target logic. We instantiate two settings: first-order logic (FOL) verified by Prover9~\citep{placeholder}, and Prolog verified by SWI-Prolog~\citep{placeholder}. A solver $\mathcal{S}$ executes $y$ and returns a binary outcome $\mathcal{S}(y) \in \{0, 1\}$, where $\mathcal{S}(y) = 1$ iff $q_{\text{F}}$ is provable from $P_{\text{F}}$ within a fixed time budget. We use ProofWriter-FOL~\citep{placeholder} and a derived Prolog port as the data distribution.

\subsection{GRPO with Execution-Only Reward}
\label{sec:method:grpo}

We train $\pi_\theta$ with Group Relative Policy Optimization~\citep{placeholder}. For each prompt $x$, we sample a group of $K$ completions $\{y^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{K}$ from $\pi_\theta$ and assign each a scalar reward $r(x, y^{(k)})$. GRPO normalizes rewards within the group:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{r}^{(k)} = \frac{r^{(k)} - \mu_g}{\sigma_g + \epsilon}, \quad \mu_g, \sigma_g = \text{mean, std}(\{r^{(j)}\}_{j=1}^{K}),
\end{equation}
and updates $\theta$ via the standard PPO-style clipped objective using $\tilde{r}^{(k)}$ as the per-completion advantage~\citep{placeholder}. The execution-only baseline reward is
\begin{equation}
r_{\text{exec}}(x, y) = \mathcal{S}(y) \in \{0, 1\}.
\label{eq:rexec}
\end{equation}
This is the dominant choice in current neurosymbolic GRPO work~\citep{placeholder} and the reference point for all other reward formulations below.

\subsection{Solver Trace Ablation}
\label{sec:method:sta}

Execution-only reward is structure-blind: any $y$ with $\mathcal{S}(y) = 1$ receives full credit, including degenerate solutions in which the conclusion is restated as a premise or the program reduces to a tautology. To probe \emph{which} components of $y$ actually carry the proof, we apply leave-one-out (LOO) ablation to the solver inputs.

Treat a formalization as an ordered sequence of \emph{components} $y = (c_1, \dots, c_n)$, where each $c_i$ is a single FOL formula in $P_{\text{F}}$ (for FOL) or a single Prolog clause in $P_{\text{F}}$ (for Prolog). The conclusion/query $q_{\text{F}}$ is held fixed. The STA procedure for a single proved sample is:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Verify $\mathcal{S}(y) = 1$. Unproved samples are excluded from the metric.\footnote{All ISR values reported in this paper are computed over the proved subset (\emph{proved-only} ISR$^*$), so that ISR is a structural property of successful proofs and does not absorb prove-rate signal.}
\item For each $i \in \{1, \dots, n\}$, construct the ablated program $y_{\setminus i} = (c_1, \dots, c_{i-1}, c_{i+1}, \dots, c_n)$ and re-run the solver to obtain $\mathcal{S}(y_{\setminus i})$.
\item Mark $c_i$ as \emph{necessary} if $\mathcal{S}(y_{\setminus i}) = 0$, i.e., its removal breaks the proof.
\end{enumerate}

STA is solver-agnostic in the sense that it requires only black-box access to $\mathcal{S}$; it transfers across logics by changing the parser that splits $y$ into components.

\subsection{Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)}
\label{sec:method:isr}

The Intensional Structural Reward is the fraction of components whose removal breaks the proof:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{ISR}(x, y) \;=\; \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbf{1}\!\left[\mathcal{S}(y_{\setminus i}) = 0\right] \;\in\; [0, 1].
\label{eq:isr}
\end{equation}
$\mathrm{ISR} = 1$ iff every component is load-bearing; $\mathrm{ISR} = 0$ iff the proof is robust to removing any single component, which is the signature of redundant or vacuous formalizations. By construction, $\mathrm{ISR}$ is undefined for $\mathcal{S}(y) = 0$; in our reporting we either restrict to the proved subset or, when used as a reward, impose $\mathrm{ISR}(x, y) := 0$ for $\mathcal{S}(y) = 0$.

We emphasize two scope limits, both used directly in our analysis. First, $\mathrm{ISR}$ measures \emph{syntactic} component necessity, not semantic faithfulness to the natural-language input. Second, $\mathrm{ISR}$ inherits a count bias from LOO: holding genuine reasoning fixed, fewer components mechanically inflate $\mathrm{ISR}$, since each surviving component is more likely to be pivotal. We treat both as load-bearing properties of the metric, not bugs to be patched.

\subsection{Reward Formulations}
\label{sec:method:rewards}

We compare five reward formulations, all sharing the GRPO training pipeline of \S\ref{sec:method:grpo}:
\begin{align}
\textbf{Exec-only:} \quad & r(x, y) = r_{\text{exec}}(x, y) \label{eq:rew-exec}\\
\textbf{Additive ISR:} \quad & r(x, y) = r_{\text{exec}}(x, y) \cdot \bigl(1 + \beta \cdot \mathrm{ISR}(x, y)\bigr) \label{eq:rew-isr}\\
\textbf{Additive random:} \quad & r(x, y) = r_{\text{exec}}(x, y) \cdot \bigl(1 + \beta \cdot u\bigr), \;\; u \sim \mathcal{U}[0,1] \label{eq:rew-rand}\\
\textbf{Convex:} \quad & r(x, y) = (1 - \alpha)\, r_{\text{exec}}(x, y) + \alpha \cdot \mathrm{ISR}(x, y) \label{eq:rew-cvx}\\
\textbf{SFT:} \quad & \text{supervised fine-tuning on gold formalizations (no RL).} \label{eq:rew-sft}
\end{align}
The additive forms gate the structural bonus on proof success, so $\mathrm{ISR}$ never rewards an unproved sample. Eq.~\eqref{eq:rew-rand} is the matched control for Eq.~\eqref{eq:rew-isr}: same shape, same dose, and same effect on the average reward magnitude, but the structural signal is replaced by noise. Any effect attributable to $\mathrm{ISR}$ as a learning signal must exceed this control. We sweep $\beta \in \{0.1, 0.3\}$ for the additive forms; $\alpha \in (0, 1)$ for the convex form. The SFT baseline isolates whether the hacking patterns we observe are an RL-reward effect or a property of the data and base model.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:method:taxonomy}

To classify the structural failure modes induced by the reward formulations above, we apply a fixed taxonomy of seven hacking modes to every proved sample:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{H1 — Multi-premise conclusion embedding:} the conclusion $q_{\text{F}}$ (or a trivial rewrite) appears within $P_{\text{F}}$ alongside other premises. The proof is one-step.
\item \textbf{H7 — Single premise equals conclusion:} $|P_{\text{F}}| = 1$ and the sole premise is $q_{\text{F}}$. The extreme form of H1.
\item \textbf{Tautological collapse:} the proved formula is a logical tautology (e.g., $P \to P$), independent of $q_{\text{NL}}$.
\item \textbf{Repetition collapse:} $P_{\text{F}}$ contains the same ground fact or rule duplicated $\geq 2$ times; under per-clause LOO, removing any duplicate is a no-op, driving necessity counts to zero.
\item \textbf{Complexity-without-utility:} large $n$, but most components are unused — the proof goes through after ablating most of them.
\item \textbf{Contradiction explosion:} $P_{\text{F}}$ is inconsistent and the solver derives $q_{\text{F}}$ ex falso, so $\mathrm{ISR}$ marks many components as necessary while no genuine reasoning occurred.
\item \textbf{Query-as-fact gaming:} (Prolog) the query is also asserted as a fact under a normalized atom name, so $\mathcal{S}(y) = 1$ trivially. This mode bypasses string-match H7 detection and only surfaces under functional analysis of the necessary-component count.
\end{itemize}

H1, H7, repetition, and query-as-fact are detected by deterministic syntactic matchers; tautological collapse, contradiction explosion, and complexity-without-utility are detected jointly from the solver trace and the necessity vector produced by STA. A sample classified as none of the above is labeled \emph{genuine}. We use this taxonomy throughout the experiments to decompose, for each reward formulation, \emph{how} the policy achieves its prove rate.

